Notebook[{
Cell["\<\
Are there preferred places for the Open and the Close in the range of a \
candlestick?\
\>", "Title",ExpressionUUID->"7a0b1926-0572-e04d-ae1d-ab250df881b4"],

Cell["\<\
A study of 519 stocks (daily, weekly and monthly candlesticks)\
\>", "Subtitle",ExpressionUUID->"c4a562c0-b06e-f644-948d-38a8fd2c7581"],

Cell["\<\
For every candlestick, the position of its Open in its range is (Open - \
Low)/(High - Low): 0 when the Open is at the Low, 1 when it is at the High. \
The same for the Close. This notebook asks whether some positions are \
preferred, using every single candlestick of 519 stocks (the constituents of \
the S&P 500 in November 2026, listed in Tickers.txt). The price data are not \
included in the package: section 2 builds them from your own access to \
FinancialData and saves in the folder Data/ only the positions, the relative \
ranges and the years (no prices). Keep the folders of the package together \
(Kernel/, Data/, Notebooks/): the paths in the cells are relative to this \
notebook.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"22abce61-de0d-d044-aef5-7bc741539c7f"],

Cell["\<\
Three things to know before reading the pictures. (1) The histogram of a \
random walk is U-shaped, not flat, because the Low and the High are places \
the price has been; so the reference in the pictures is a simulated random \
walk (the orange line), not a flat line. (2) Before 2001 prices were quoted \
in fractions, and a candlestick with a range of 2, 3 or 4 ticks can only have \
positions 1/2, 1/3, 1/4, ...: that makes sharp spikes that are the price \
grid, not a preference (section 5 shows them), so the analysis starts in 2001 \
and, for daily candlesticks, keeps only those whose range is at least 3 % of \
the price. (3) A position of exactly 0 or exactly 1 (the Open at the Low, the \
Close at the High, ...) is a point mass and is reported on its own.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"023bb7b8-1d12-d54a-8349-a87cbad255d6"],

Cell["\<\
How to run: evaluate section 1, then section 2 once (it takes hours for all \
the stocks; try 10 tickers first). Then sections 3 to 5 are independent of \
each other, except that section 5 uses weeklyCollected from section 3. Every \
call spells out all its options, with the values used for the results in the \
post, so you can change them. The numbers in the post come from the 519 \
tickers and the prices of November 2026; a build on another day, or with \
fewer tickers, gives slightly different (and, with few tickers, noisier) \
numbers. It is a description of where prices end up inside their range, not a \
trading signal, and not investment advice.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"6784d42d-c85b-1c4d-9f9c-045bf40f0601"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["1. Load the code", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"e0040458-14e4-0c44-8e85-6f5627feeacb"],

Cell["\<\
The file Kernel/RangePositionPreferences.wl defines buildRangePositionData \
(makes the saved data from FinancialData), collectRangePositions (reads the \
positions of the candlesticks), rangePositionPreferences (the histograms, the \
random walk, the spikes) and showRangePositionPreferences (the pictures). \
Each has a usage message: evaluate ?buildRangePositionData, \
?collectRangePositions, ?rangePositionPreferences, \
?showRangePositionPreferences. Its tests are in \
Tests/RangePositionPreferences.wlt.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"932bf95a-e1c4-3c4b-b872-fd683d4f3f9d"],

